Initially, dry eye may be a relatively minor problem, but it can cause serious difficulty for some people. Dry eye may begin at any age and tends to get worse as we age.
Symptoms caused by dry eye can include burning, irritation and itching, as well as tearing or watering eyes. You might not expect tearing to be a problem in patients with dry eye.
Other dry eye symptoms may include eye pain or blurred vision. One clue that blurred vision is due to dry eye is that it clears temporarily after blinking.
Patients with severe dry eyes are usually prescribed preservative-free artificial tears. This helps reduce side effects such as toxicity that can develop when the tears are used more frequently than 4-6 times a day.
Dry eye ointments may be appropriate for people who need additional lubrication at night.
When artificial tears do not reduce the symptoms of dry eyes, other medication such as topical cyclosporin (Restasis) or topical steroids may be prescribed.
Sometimes dry eyes can be associated with blepharitis or meibomitis (eyelid irritation). Your doctor may prescribe therapies for these problems in conjunction with dry eye treatment.
For severe dry eyes, a procedure can be done to block the system of canals (lacrimal system) that normally drain tears away from the surface of the eye. This is the equivalent of putting a plug in the drain of a sink.
If permanent closure of the lacrimal punctae is needed, a surgical procedure to cauterize the punctae may be performed.
The management of dry eye can vary significantly from patient to patient, and we have more treatment choices than ever before. Consult your eye doctor to determine what treatment program will be the most appropriate for you.
