Baobab oil is rich in omega-3 fatty acids and other fats, including:
In fact, linoleic and linolenic acids are some of the most widely used fatty acids in skin care, cosmetics and hair care worldwide. Baobab oil can also help treat eczema.
In areas where the baobab grows, the locals use every part of the tree. The fruit, leaves, seeds, and oil extracted from the seeds are used in nutritional supplements, food, and skin care.
A clinical trial has shown that baobab oil can help improve acne in some people. This is because it is high in linoleic acid, a type of omega-3 fatty acid that has anti-inflammatory properties.
Baobab fruit and oil do not contain much protein. The oil may not repair damaged hair like other protein-rich hair products can my.
It is not known how much of the nutrients from the baobab fruit and seeds remain in the oil after the oil is cold pressed or stored.
If you have very oily, acne-prone or sensitive skin, use baobab oil sparingly. It can clog pores in some people, which can cause or worsen acne.
Know the name of the ingredient. Check hair and skin care products for baobab oil.
Look for cold pressed. If you are buying pure baobab oil, look for cold-pressed and unrefined oil.
If you are using baobab oil for the first time on your skin, hair or scalp, do a patch test. Apply a small amount of oil to the inside of the elbow and wait 24 hours.
Every part of the baobab tree has been researched and tested for its many nutritional benefits. Baobab fruit is included in many nutritional supplements because it contains many important vitamins and minerals.
