Prior to the first corona crisis in 2020, supplies of materials and raw materials in China were a fairly predictable and simple process. The prices of goods, of course, varied from supplier to supplier, and the higher the quantity ordered, the lower the purchase price.
We have witnessed the closure of public life, the mandatory wearing of protective masks and disinfection of hands at every turn, the closure of shops and schools, quarantines, the ban on crossing municipal borders, there have been a huge number of fatalities. The crisis has closed the economy around the world, but not in China, where the covid was completed after consistent quarantines a few weeks after the Chinese New Year celebrations, in the spring of 2020.
Towards the end of 2020, the first collapse followed, when China began to run short of shipping containers, as they were unable to return quickly enough from around the world to which they had previously been shipped.
As said, towards the end of 2020, also due to the approaching New Year holidays around the world in late December 2020 and the one-month celebration of Chinese New Year in February 2021, it was virtually impossible to get a free container in China, let alone shipping space. Long rows of trucks piled up in front of Chinese ports.
However, others had problems, and considerable ones: they also waited for more than 2 months for the container to be shipped, as European freight forwarders simply could not load them on ships. And fare prices skyrocketed, transporting a 40 ft container to Europe soon cost between $ 9.000 USD and $ 10.000 USD, transporting 20 ft. the container is between $ 5.000 USD and $ 6.000 USD.
The shortage of raw materials has led to higher food prices, real estate prices have risen and Europe is on the verge of inflation, which is already knocking on the door.
For some time, rail transport was the only rational alternative to shipping, it was slightly more expensive but also slightly faster than shipping. Unfortunately, it is no longer an alternative as there are major congestion and high prices as well as delays as it has become slower than shipping.
In the given situation, our company has slightly adjusted and focused on deliveries from reliable suppliers in Europe as well as in Russia and India, which means that we can compete a lot in terms of price and speed of delivery of packaging and materials. We have even gone so far as to become a supplier to our existing Chinese suppliers for their European customers due to competitive prices, fast delivery and quality products!
